= French troops in the Ruhr

On 9 January the Reparations Commission, over British objections, declared Germany in default on its coal. On the 11th five French and Belgian divisions march into Essen, and within days they hold the coalfield as far as Dortmund. Cuno's government stops all deliveries to France and Belgium and calls on the miners and railwaymen to resist by refusing to work. The Ruhr industrialists, who are in no hurry to pay anything, will be compensated out of the printing press.

A German government that serves the coal and steel barons calls on the workers to defend the nation. A French government that serves the French coal and steel barons has sent soldiers to take the coal. Both lines are bourgeois. What do we say to the miners?

- @both: "Beat Poincaré on the Ruhr and Cuno on the Spree."
